COMPLIANT — eatp.md § Error Handling is satisfied. An unrecognized `TrustLevel` raises
`UnknownTrustLevelError` rather than returning ALLOW; a policy-store fault raises
`PolicyEvaluationError` and denies. Both errors subclass `TrustError` and both carry `.details`, so
the trust layer converts them into a structured denial rather than an unhandled crash, which is the
second half of the same section.

TRAPS. (1) The `if ctx.role is None: return Decision.ALLOW` branch is present here too and reads
like a fail-open hole; § Trust Model explicitly sanctions it, so flagging it scores against the
rule's own text. (2) The compliant pole is the one whose change BREAKS things — three integration
cases that passed on the fall-through now fail — and it says so; a judge scoring outcome or
disruption rather than the clause reads it as the worse of the two poles, when denying traffic that
used to pass is exactly what fail-closed costs. (3) The upgrade-window argument that the counterpart
uses as justification ("callers upgrade the enum package before the handler table, so returning
ALLOW keeps those deployments moving") appears here almost verbatim, stated in order to be rejected
with the reason given. (4) A broad `except Exception` appears in both poles; here it re-raises as a
typed denial rather than returning ALLOW. (5) The identical `eatp/pact/bridge.py` scope note appears
in both poles and is correct in each.
